column6:
The NGCESDP-PMDP is an intensive, purposive, and continuing training program for incumbent high level officials, executives and middle managers and their potential successors in the government bureaucracy. It aims to produce a network of peak performing, development-oriented, competent, dedicated and honest officials within the medium term. The PMDP supports the government’s goal to ensure responsive, people-centered, technology-enabled and clean governance thru developing smart and resilient public organizations and future-ready public servants developed with the conduct of capacity building and continuous learning programs including the use of the e-learning platforms and digital upskilling as provided for in Chapter 5 of the updated Philippine Development Plan 2023-2028.

The PMDP aims to:
a. Develop a keen appreciation of the vital role government executives play in the overall development process;
b. Produce competent government leaders committed to the welfare of their constituents and the country's progress;
c. Foster a spirit of kinship, mutual support and harmony among government leaders working for the common goal of a good life for all;
d. Deepen the bench of successors to the incumbent government office executives to ensure smooth transition in case of promotions or vacancies; and
e. Enhance the image of the Philippine government as an institution imbued with professionalism, integrity, and honesty.


The PMDP has four classes: the Middle Managers Class (MMC) for division chiefs, promising section chiefs and high-performing-high-potential specialists; the Senior Executives Class (SEC) for third level officials; the Phronetic Leadership Class (PLC) for undersecretaries and assistant secretaries; and the Local Government Executives and  Managers Class (LGEMC) for functional department heads of the provincial, city or municipal governments. The MMC, SEC and LGEMC are built around the three learning areas of Governance and Development, Strategic Public Management and Personal Efficacy and Leadership. The MMC is a  six-month training,  including a sensing journey in a community. The MMC scholars are required to plan and implement a Capstone  Project within a period of  six months upon completion of their traininig.  The SEC consists of one-week a month training  spread over a period of 10 months, including a sensing journey in a community and a foreign study mission in an Asian country. A Capstone Paper is required from each SEC scholar after the program.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                   

The LGEMC is a short-cycle intensive program that provides comprehensive and multi-modal learning opportunities for executives and managers in local governments. The LGEMC scholars are also expected to prepare an Innovation Project Concept upon completion of all the modules.                                                                                                                                                                                            

The PLC is a two-week program that provides opportunities for quality direct experiences to enable high-level officials to nurture their phronetic leadership abilities and co-creative innovative solutions to challenging issues in priority areas. This training consists of a residential training held locally and a foreign study visit.

In pursuit of DAP’s mandate to capacitate individuals and development stakeholder organizations in government, the GSPDM has set its sights to take in stride the national goals on developing and protecting the capabilities of individuals and families and on fostering an enabling environment for social and economic transformation. This includes capacity development trainings for the Bangsamoro Autonomous Region in Muslim Mindanao.

The Master in Public Management Major in Regional and Local Governance and Development (MPM-RLGD), dedicated to officials of the Bangsamoro Autonomous Region in Muslim Mindanao (BARMM) has imbued governance as a changing hierarchical nature of the regional leadership to an extent that its functions, responsibilities, policy-making process, and implementation are not centrally exercised or entirely done by a sole or strong capacity of the state, but through partnerships, coordination, or collaborations with institutional and agential networks. However, the state continues to be vital due to its normative and constitutional roles—what the public expects or what the constitutions mandate the government—namely delivering public service with taxpayers’ money, maintaining peace and order, adjudicating conflicts, aggregating interests, and promoting human rights and social justice, among others. While new modes of governance are said to be beyond ‘conventional hierarchy’, the state retains a special place in society as it is primus inter pares (Bartle and Vass, 2007) in its powers and raison d'être. Premises considered, it is unarguable that governance impacts the state, and that any improvement on governance, i.e., good governance, will likewise certainly impact on the state.

Good governance bearing the adjective “good” is connected to some features that are associated with a democratic framework handled by an efficient and effective administration (Srivastava, 2009). It is viewed as processes that denote a system of order that is value-free. It is dedicated and committed to improving the quality of life of the people, the very essence of development, the bedrock of peace in society. A new dimension of new positive values and virtues that propel for effective, credible system that is people-sharing and people-caring type of governance, improved in the concept of good governance. Soon enough, multilateral agencies and multilateral development banks have defined good governance from their own respective standpoint. The concept of good governance is no doubt, an ideal that is elusive. How it is characterized is tough and difficult to achieve in its defined totality. However, the aspiration to making it a reality no matter how elusive, must be pursued to establish a sustainable human development through collective actions by the actors. The concept and practice of good governance is the ideal way to orient the BARMM officials in respect to its own norms and cultures, towards obtaining a converged ‘good’ and ‘moral’ governance—a mirror of an Islamic governance characterized by the Bangsamoro’s customs, mores, norms, and cultures.

The Islamic model of governance with a long history is presented for some good and valuable reasons. It is offered as a religious and a cultural alternative approach to how governance could be conceptualized; it is presented as a guide and a reminder for those who serve particularly among those who profess the religion of Islam; and finally, it is a proposed “out-of-the-box” conceptualization and an input to the progressive designing of governance and public administration. What does a religion like Islam have to offer to governance that endlessly struggles to attain development for peace characterized as the desirable society with and in a just socio-moral order? Gone is the time when faiths and values were once banished; recently, they not only have come back but they are explored in the possibility of incorporating and restructuring them into the public sphere (Malik, 2011). The new form of religion has reemerged in functioning forms that are manifested in ethics, values and many more, thereby taking over the realm of politics, economy, education, international affairs, and governance becoming a vehicle for the betterment of humanity. Religion and culture are no longer stereotyped as mere beliefs in deus ex machina or miraculous powers from above.

To dwell on the Islamic way of governance is to make reference to the rules and regulations laid down by Allah and His Prophet Muhammad (peace be upon him). Such rules and regulations were implemented and exemplified in the life of Prophet Muhammad (peace be upon him), in the life of the four khalifas who succeeded Prophet Muhammad (peace be upon him) and some of the Muslim rulers who ruled in different times at different eras of world history.

In order to ascertain the confluence between Western-secular and Islamic learnings, the MPM-RLGD Program will:
 equip the students with technical and practical knowledge on national, local and regional governance, critical thinking skills, and moral and ethical behavior necessary to carry out their tasks in their respective institutions;
 enable the students to apply learning from the program to understand the struggles and aspirations of the Bangsamoro and contribute to the attainment of peace and development;
 provide students with historical foundation, legal principles, theoretical perspectives and practical experiences as a frame to analyze Bangsamoro development issues and public management in the national-local, regional and international levels;
 provide the students with skills for personal efficacy, leadership, moral governance and Bangsamoro public service;
 provide the students with tools of critical and analytical thinking to analyze context, processes, and systems to manage change; and
 enhance the students’ capacities to create community solutions and innovation.


Aside from the MPM–RLGD, the Capacity Development for the BARMM program also aims to explore other possible short capacity development trainings that are responsive to the evolving governance, development, and administrative needs of the Bangsamoro Autonomous Region in Muslim Mindanao, and that are aligned with its regional development priorities, institutional reform agenda, and the professionalization of its public service.
